# require-per-env.config — tflw `M266`: `C131` (`config:key:require`).
#
# A `require env` line inside an `env` block. `env two` requires `C131_TOKEN` and reads it into a
# header; `env one` neither requires it nor reads it. The row's claim is that the requirement belongs
# to the block it is written in: under `env one` nothing asks for the variable, under `env two` the
# run is refused before its first request and the refusal says which env asked.
#
# `api` points at port 9 for `require.config`'s reason — on the fetch standard's blocked-ports list,
# so the leg that gets past the gate fails with "no socket was opened" whether or not apiV2 is up, and
# "refused before it started" is told from "ran and failed" without a stack.

env one default
  api "http://127.0.0.1:9/v1"

env two
  api "http://127.0.0.1:9/v1"
  require env C131_TOKEN
  header "X-C131" is env(C131_TOKEN)
